## Lifecycle and Ownership

All identity arguments require replacement when changed; `privileges` updates in place and accepts only `SELECT`.
Before each change the resource checks that the database, schema, table, and policy exist (`SVV_RLS_POLICY` or
`SVV_MASKING_POLICY`); a missing parent removes the resource from state. Deleting the resource revokes the grant.
Policies cannot hold grant options.

**Redshift does not report grants to policies.** The AWS reference lists only users, roles, groups, and `PUBLIC` as the
identities that [SHOW GRANTS](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SHOW_GRANTS.html) and
[SVV_RELATION_PRIVILEGES](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/latest/dg/r_SVV_RELATION_PRIVILEGES.html) return, so
the provider cannot read the grant back. State records what Terraform granted: Create and Update run `GRANT` or `REVOKE`
for the difference between the recorded and the configured `privileges`, refresh keeps the recorded value, and Delete
revokes it. A grant revoked or added outside Terraform is not detected; change `privileges` or replace the resource to
apply it again. For the same reason there is no `redshift_policy_grant` data source.

